    <!-- ═══ SECTION 8 — DEFENSE ═══ -->
    <div class="section" id="s8">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">Section 08</div>
      <div class="section-title">Defense in depth</div>
      <div class="section-intro">Defense in depth is a strategy that uses multiple layers of security to protect data.
        If one layer fails, another is in place to stop the attacker.</div>

      <div class="layers-container">
        <div class="layer-item">
          <div class="layer-head"><span class="layer-tag">Layer 1</span><span class="layer-name">Perimeter</span></div>
          <div class="layer-body">
            <div class="layer-desc">This is the outermost edge of the network. It focuses on blocking external threats
              before they ever enter the internal environment.</div>
            <div class="layer-row"><strong>Tools:</strong><span>Firewalls, Intrusion Detection/Prevention Systems
                (IDS/IPS), and DDoS mitigation.</span></div>
            <div class="layer-row"><strong>Goal:</strong><span>To filter out malicious traffic and "noise" from the open
                internet.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-item">
          <div class="layer-head"><span class="layer-tag">Layer 2</span><span class="layer-name">Network</span></div>
          <div class="layer-body">
            <div class="layer-desc">Once inside the perimeter, the network itself must be secured to prevent attackers
              from moving freely.</div>
            <div class="layer-row"><strong>Tools:</strong><span>VPNs for secure remote access and Network Segmentation
                (breaking the network into smaller, isolated zones).</span></div>
            <div class="layer-row"><strong>Strategy:</strong><span>Zero Trust Architecture, which operates on the
                principle of "never trust, always verify."</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-item">
          <div class="layer-head"><span class="layer-tag">Layer 3</span><span class="layer-name">Endpoint</span></div>
          <div class="layer-body">
            <div class="layer-desc">This layer protects individual devices—such as laptops, desktops, and servers—where
              users interact with data.</div>
            <div class="layer-row"><strong>Tools:</strong><span>Antivirus (AV), Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR),
                and Patch Management (keeping software updated to fix vulnerabilities).</span></div>
            <div class="layer-row"><strong>Goal:</strong><span>To stop malware like ransomware or spyware from executing
                on a specific machine.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-item">
          <div class="layer-head"><span class="layer-tag">Layer 4</span><span class="layer-name">Application</span>
          </div>
          <div class="layer-body">
            <div class="layer-desc">Even on a secure device, the specific software programs (apps) being used can have
              flaws.</div>
            <div class="layer-row"><strong>Tools:</strong><span>Web Application Firewalls (WAF) and Penetration
                Testing.</span></div>
            <div class="layer-row"><strong>Process:</strong><span>Using a Secure SDLC (Software Development Life Cycle)
                to ensure code is written safely from the start.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-item">
          <div class="layer-head"><span class="layer-tag">Layer 5</span><span class="layer-name">Data</span></div>
          <div class="layer-body">
            <div class="layer-desc">This is the "crown jewel" layer. If an attacker bypasses every other defense, this
              layer ensures the data itself remains unusable or protected.</div>
            <div class="layer-row"><strong>Tools:</strong><span>Encryption (so stolen data can't be read), Data Loss
                Prevention (DLP), and Backups (to recover from ransomware).</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-item">
          <div class="layer-head"><span class="layer-tag">Layer 6</span><span class="layer-name">Human</span></div>
          <div class="layer-body">
            <div class="layer-desc">The innermost layer recognizes that people are often the primary target through
              social engineering.</div>
            <div class="layer-row"><strong>Tools:</strong><span>Security awareness training and Multi-Factor
                Authentication (MFA).</span></div>
            <div class="layer-row"><strong>Goal:</strong><span>To turn employees into a "human firewall" who can
                recognize phishing attempts.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

