          <div class="q-opt" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">D</span>Quid pro quo</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q-feedback show fb-correct" id="q2-fb">Correct — whaling is spear phishing aimed specifically at high-value executives. Combined with impersonating the CEO to trigger a wire transfer, this is also known as a Business Email Compromise (BEC) attack.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="q-item" id="q3">
        <div class="q-num">Question 03 / 05</div>
        <div class="q-text">A developer forgets to sanitize a login form. An attacker types <code>' OR '1'='1</code>
          into the username field and gains admin access without knowing the password. What vulnerability is exploited?
        </div>
        <div class="q-opts">
          <div class="q-opt" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">A</span>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
          </div>
          <div class="q-opt" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">B</span>Cross-Site Request Forgery
            (CSRF)</div>
          <div class="q-opt correct" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">C</span>SQL Injection</div>
          <div class="q-opt" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">D</span>Buffer overflow</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q-feedback show fb-correct" id="q3-fb">Correct — the payload ' OR '1'='1 manipulates the SQL query so the WHERE clause always evaluates to TRUE, bypassing authentication. This is the classic SQL injection signature.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="q-item" id="q4">
        <div class="q-num">Question 04 / 05</div>
        <div class="q-text">WannaCry encrypted files on 200,000 computers across 150 countries and demanded Bitcoin for
          decryption keys. Which malware category best describes it?</div>
        <div class="q-opts">
          <div class="q-opt" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">A</span>Spyware</div>
          <div class="q-opt" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">B</span>Rootkit</div>
          <div class="q-opt" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">C</span>Adware</div>
          <div class="q-opt correct" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">D</span>Ransomware</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q-feedback show fb-correct" id="q4-fb">Correct — WannaCry encrypted files and demanded cryptocurrency for the decryption key, which is the definition of ransomware. It also spread autonomously using the EternalBlue exploit, making it a ransomware worm.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="q-item" id="q5">
        <div class="q-num">Question 05 / 05</div>
        <div class="q-text">The SolarWinds attack compromised thousands of organizations by inserting malicious code
          into a trusted software update that customers downloaded themselves. What type of attack is this?</div>
        <div class="q-opts">
          <div class="q-opt" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">A</span>Zero-day exploit</div>
          <div class="q-opt correct" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">B</span>Supply chain attack</div>
          <div class="q-opt" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">C</span>Credential stuffing</div>
          <div class="q-opt" style="pointer-events: none;"><span class="opt-letter">D</span>DNS spoofing</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q-feedback show fb-correct" id="q5-fb">Correct — compromising a trusted vendor's software before delivery to customers is a supply chain attack. SolarWinds is the most famous modern example, affecting 18,000+ organizations.</div>
      </div>

